From 3edf82a876f8da09c767ca9530d3857d846b0bcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:44:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GBM: HDR10 adapted to the display, the decision published
 once it is written (R-A1)

Hdr10AdaptWanted() marked the decision of a video as made
(m_hdr10AdaptDecided.exchange(true)) before it wrote it, and the
decision, its mode and the time of the last picture drawn so were read
with no synchronisation: by another thread that asks the same question
(the renderer asks it from VideoPlayer's thread, DVBridgeConfigChanged(),
and from the render thread, Configure()), and by the player information.
A caller could read a verdict half written; formally, undefined
behaviour. Found by the second independent review of 1.0 RC6 (R-A1); not
reproduced (it needs Kodi's two threads).

HDR10_ADAPT::CState holds the decision of a video in one atomic word,
with its state and the video it is of: the first caller of a video
decides in local and publishes it with release; a caller that comes
while it is being decided computes its own in local and uses it without
publishing it; once it is published every caller and reader takes it
(acquire). A new video (Reset()) makes the next caller decide again, and
a decision begun before it is not published for the new video. There is
no data beside the word to be read half written: it holds what the
readers use (the verdict, the mode, whether the video's peak is its
MaxCLL, that peak rounded to cd/m2 as it is shown, and the display's as
the float the mapping takes, so the mapping is the same).

TakeHdr10AdaptMapping() takes the decision the same way, so a renderer
whose question was answered in local maps with the same peak. The line
of every 10 s is begun again on the render thread, where it is kept,
when the video it is of changes; the time of the last picture drawn so
is an atomic. The decision is told in kodi.log once, by the caller that
published it.

A host test runs the class in threads, also with ThreadSanitizer (clean),
and the shape of the code before the fix in the same threads, which
ThreadSanitizer reports.
